# Source of truth for the runtime dependencies. requirements.txt is GENERATED
# from this file and pins every package, including transitive ones — edit this
# file, never that one, then regenerate:
#
#   uv pip compile requirements.in --universal --python-version 3.12 --generate-hashes -o requirements.txt
#   uv pip compile requirements-dev.in --universal --python-version 3.12 -c requirements.txt --generate-hashes -o requirements-dev.txt
#
# --universal keeps the environment markers, so one lock serves Linux (CI and
# Vercel) and Windows (local) — uvloop stays excluded on win32, colorama stays
# included. --python-version 3.12 matches .python-version and CI; resolving on
# the local 3.14 would pin versions production never sees.
#
# --generate-hashes records a SHA-256 for every artifact, which is what makes a
# pinned version name a particular file rather than whatever the index serves
# under that version today. A pin alone is only as good as the index: the
# threat it closes is a compromised or substituted mirror, on top of TLS.
#
# Three things follow, and all three are load-bearing:
#
#   - Hashes are all-or-nothing. pip switches hash checking on by itself the
#     moment it sees one, and then refuses every requirement that lacks one.
#     So both locks carry them or neither does, and a package hand-added to
#     either file fails the install instead of slipping in unnoticed. That is
#     the point, but it also means regenerating without the flag breaks the
#     build rather than quietly weakening it.
#   - The `locks` job in ci.yml recompiles these files and diffs the result,
#     so it passes the same flag. Drop it in one place and CI reddens.
#   - uv verifies a hash it finds without being asked, so Vercel (which
#     builds with uv, not pip) enforces these too, with no flag set.
#     Do NOT add UV_REQUIRE_HASHES to vercel.json to make that explicit:
#     it is an environment variable, so it governs Vercel's own install
#     of `vercel-runtime` as well, which ships no hashes, and the build
#     dies before it ever reads this file. Tried on 2026-09-15 -- the
#     preview failed with "none were provided for: vercel-runtime==0.23.0".
#
# On a machine whose antivirus intercepts HTTPS (Norton, here), uv rejects the
# substituted certificate — add --native-tls to read the OS trust store. Same
# root cause as api/_src/dev_loop.py.
